id: recipe.redis-backed-service
kind: recipe
title: Service using Redis
aliases: redis design, cache and counters with redis
question: how would you design a service that uses Redis?
tags: architecture, caching, state
properties:
  - reads decoupled from the database
  - bounded staleness via TTL + invalidation
  - atomic counters and leases in a shared store
  - explicit behaviour when Redis is empty or slow
invariant: Redis stores derived, reconstructible state (cache, counters, sessions, locks), never the only copy of data you cannot rebuild.
answer_30s: Split Redis by role: a cache instance with TTLs and eviction, and a separate coordination instance for counters and locks, with a plan for cold-start and latency spikes.
flow: |
  service replicas
     |
  cache role: entries with TTL (allkeys-lru)   coordination role: counters, locks, rate limits
     |
  database / primary source of truth (durable)
     |
  failure: cache loss = slower, not broken; coordination loss = degraded, not corrupt
compose:
  - design.redis-cache => what Redis is good at and its operating limits
  - design.cache-aside => read path and invalidation rules
  - design.cache-invalidation => TTL, versioned keys, pub/sub purge
  - design.rate-limiting => atomic counters (INCR/Lua) as a limiter
  - design.distributed-locking => leases and the fencing-token caveat
  - design.idempotency => idempotency keys stored with a TTL
  - design.backpressure => a cache miss storm must not take down the database
  - design.observability => hit ratio, evictions, latency percentiles, memory
  - k8s.service => stable address for the Redis endpoint
  - k8s.pvc => persistence decision for the coordination instance
failure_modes:
  - cache treated as authoritative, so a flush becomes data loss
  - one instance for cache and locks, so eviction silently breaks coordination
  - no memory limit or eviction policy, so the instance is OOM-killed under pressure
  - big keys or KEYS/SCAN on the hot path, spiking latency for everyone
  - session state with no TTL, so memory grows without bound
follow_ups:
  - Redis restarts empty: what happens to your service in the first minute?
related:
  - design.redis-cache
  - design.cache-aside
  - design.rate-limiting
sources:
  - https://redis.io/docs/manual/patterns/
  - https://redis.io/docs/reference/optimization/latency/
